_model: proposition
---
title: Proposition 7
---
order: 7
---
short_label: VI.7
---
sections:

#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
statement: If two triangles have one angle equal to one angle, the sides about other angles proportional, and the remaining angles either both less or both not less than a right angle, then the triangles are equiangular and have those angles equal the sides about which are proportional.
----
proof:

Let {ABC|ABCt} and {DEF|DEFt} be two triangles having one angle equal to one angle, the angle {BAC|angA} equal to the angle {EDF|angD}, the sides about other angles {ABC|angABC} and {DEF|angDEF} proportional, so that {AB} is to {BC} as {DE} is to {EF}. And, first, each of the remaining angles at {C|angC} and {F|angF} less than a right angle.

I say that the triangle {ABC|ABCt} is equiangular with the triangle {DEF|DEFt}, the angle {ABC|angABC} equals the angle {DEF|angDEF}, and the remaining angle, namely the angle at {C|angC}, equals the remaining angle, the angle at {F|angF}.

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="340" height="280" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="VI.7 diagram (a)" src="propVI7a.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    // Two figures, one per case, each with its own walk: this one is the
    // first case (the angles at C and F less than a right angle), canvas_1
    // the second. The prose between the canvases binds to canvas_0 by DOM
    // order and everything after canvas_1 to canvas_1, which is what each
    // passage means, so no ref needs a canvas selector.
    //
    // Both cases are a reductio: G is the point the I.23 construction
    // would give if ABC were greater than DEF. Joyce's figure has it as a
    // slider on AC (F is `similar`, so ABC in fact equals DEF), and the
    // walk withdraws G, BG and their angles once the absurdity is reached
    // (III.16's close).
    //
    // Everything added is transparent: the sides AB, BC, DE, EF (;0;0;0)
    // and faceless twins of the triangles ABC, DEF, ABG (explicit ;0
    // faces). A highlighted filled polygon is raised (#140) and would cover
    // the angle markers, so captions and prose route "the triangle ABC" /
    // "DEF" to the twins with the display override. Angle and triangle
    // names collide (ABC, DEF, ABG): the triangles own them, the angles take
    // the display override.
    //
    // Markers: equal angles share a colour — A, D orange; DEF and ABG blue;
    // AGB and F green; C and BGC teal; ABC (the angle supposed greater)
    // purple. AGB and BGC lie side by side at G, pinned at 22 so they do not
    // step into rings; ABG nests inside ABC at B. None is a right angle in
    // the text ("less than" / "greater than a right angle"), so all are
    // sectors.
    //
    // Draggables (checked against the bundle): A, B, C, D, E free, given on
    // slide 1; G is a lineSlider that arrives with the construction, so it
    // is deferred. F is derived (`similar`) and listed per slide.
    var GIVEN = ["A","B","C","ABC","D","E","F","DEF",
                 "AB","BC","DE","EF","ABCt","DEFt"];
    var HYP   = GIVEN.concat(["angA","angD"]);
    var SIDES = HYP.concat(["angABC","angDEF"]);
    var ACUTE = SIDES.concat(["angC","angF"]);
    var CON   = ACUTE.concat(["angABG","BG","G","ABG"]);
    var REM   = CON.concat(["angAGB"]);
    var ISO   = REM.concat(["angBGC"]);
    geomlib.init({
