        "I.13": "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI13/",
        "I.17": "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI17/",
        "I.23": "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI23/",
        "I.32": "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI32/",
        "V.9":  "/elements/books/bookV/propositions/propV9/",
        "V.11": "/elements/books/bookV/propositions/propV11/",
        "VI.4": "/elements/books/bookVI/propositions/propVI4/"
    });
</script>
</figure>

If the angle {ABC|angABC} does not equal the angle {DEF|angDEF}, then one of them is greater.

Let the angle {ABC|angABC} be greater. Construct the angle {ABG|angABG} equal to the angle {DEF|angDEF} on the straight line {AB} and at the point {B} on it. [!just I.23]

Then, since the angle {A|angA} equals {D|angD}, and the angle {ABG|angABG} equals the angle {DEF|angDEF}, therefore the remaining angle {AGB|angAGB} equals the remaining angle {DFE|angF}. [!just I.32]

Therefore the triangle {ABG} is equiangular with the triangle {DEF|DEFt}.

Therefore {AB} is to {BG} as {DE} is to {EF}. [!just VI.4]

But, by hypothesis, {DE} is to {EF} as {AB} is to {BC}, therefore {AB} has the same ratio to each of the straight lines {BC} and {BG}. Therefore {BC} equals {BG}, so that the angle at {C|angC} also equals the angle {BGC|angBGC}. [!just V.11; V.9; I.5]

But, by hypothesis, the angle at {C|angC} is less than a right angle, therefore the angle {BGC|angBGC} is also less than a right angle, so that the angle {AGB|angAGB} adjacent to it is greater than a right angle. [!just I.13]

And it was proved equal to the angle at {F|angF}, therefore the angle at {F|angF} is also greater than a right angle. But it is by hypothesis less than a right angle, which is absurd.

Therefore the angle {ABC|angABC} is not unequal to the angle {DEF|angDEF}. Therefore it equals it.

But the angle at {A|angA} also equals the angle at {D|angD}, therefore the remaining angle at {C|angC} equals the remaining angle at {F|angF}. [!just I.32]

Therefore the triangle {ABC|ABCt} is equiangular with the triangle {DEF|DEFt}.

